An example of our dataset building approach involves enumerating sentence pairs as candidates for each document. For every pair, we calculate the Longest Common Subsequence (LCS). The pairs that have an LCS longer than the threshold are chosen as similar texts. We split the article "Tom and Jerry" into sentences and couple every two different sentences as candidate similar text pairs. For instance, the LCS of "Tom is chasing Jerry." and "Jerry is chasing Tom." is "is chasing", which contains only 9 Latin characters and is less than our threshold. Therefore, these pairs are ignored as dissimilar. The substring "Spike is chasing" is identified as the LCS among the sentences "Spike is chasing Tom." and "Spike is chasing Jerry.", which has 14 Latin characters and is included in our training dataset as a relevant pair.

